8.5 Command Units
Command units are the strategic backbone of the Warden Program.
They do not fight directly — instead, they provide command radius, bonuses, strategic abilities, and battlefield intelligence.

This section defines the eight Command Units from your chart.

Archive
Role: Long‑term data repository
Abilities: Intel boost, cooldown reduction
Bonuses: Sensor range, recon efficiency

Worldmap
Role: Strategic overview node
Abilities: Map reveal, fog‑of‑war manipulation
Bonuses: Sensor triangulation, elevation mapping

Command Core
Role: Primary HQ
Abilities: Global buffs, unit unlocks
Bonuses: Command radius, morale, initiative

Fusion
Role: Power generation
Abilities: Energy grid, shield boosts
Bonuses: Recharge rate, repair speed

Nexus
Role: Network relay
Abilities: Link squads, share sensor data
Bonuses: Coordination, multi‑angle assault timing

Insight
Role: Advanced sensor center
Abilities: Cloak detection, precision targeting
Bonuses: Visibility cones, elevation advantage

Pulse
Role: Tactical pulse emitter
Abilities: EMP bursts, disruption fields
Bonuses: Anti‑stealth, anti‑electronics

Vector Core
Role: Experimental command node
Abilities: Teleportation, vector manipulation
Bonuses: Rapid redeploy, experimental unlocks

Command units define the strategic layer of the game.

8.6 Experimental Units
Experimental units break standard rules.
They are powerful, rare, and capable of altering the battlefield itself.

Experimental Unit Mechanics
Unique abilities

Terrain manipulation

Energy fields

Teleportation

Gravity distortion

Cloaking

Superweapons

Long cooldowns

High resource cost

Experimental Unit Roles
Echo — sensor ghost, stealth manipulation

Nullpoint — gravity well, battlefield disruption

Specter — cloaked strike unit

Shadowgrid — electronic warfare

Phantom — teleportation assault

Helix — biological/chemical hazard deployment

Experimental Unit AI Behaviors
Ignore standard terrain rules

Use abilities to reshape battlefield

Coordinate with command units

Retreat when cooldowns are active

Avoid detection until strike moment

Experimental units are designed for late‑game spectacle and strategic depth.
